text:
Handoff packet

purpose, scope, stakeholders, and current status;
README and directory map;
data inventory, provenance, permissions, and retention notes;
current analysis path and software/environment notes;
key outputs with dates, versions, and interpretation limits;
decision log, open issues, and recommended next actions;
named owner for the next review.







Tip



Ask the recipient to perform an orientation walkthrough and one small reproduction check. Update the handoff note with what they could and could not recover.

Initial technical inspection
Check what is appropriate:

file count, size, readability, archive integrity, format, delimiter, and encoding;
table or layer names, row and column counts, schema, and duplicate files;
expected time range, spatial extent, CRS, raster dimensions, alignment, and geometry validity;
sequence count and metadata linkage;
missing documentation and unexpected executable or unsupported content.

Do not perform substantive cleaning in the preserved source location.

Assign roles and authority
Use roles rather than assuming a particular job title:

project lead;
data provider or steward;
analytical lead;
subject-matter reviewer;
technical reviewer;
operational owner;
release authority;
future maintainer.

One person may hold multiple roles. The purpose is to identify responsibility and decision authority, not to create unnecessary bureaucracy.






Example



A simulation project should fix scenario definitions, parameter ownership, acceptance criteria, and review authority before committing to a large HPC run. Otherwise a completed run may not answer the intended question.

Recurring strengths
Across the sample, the most reusable strengths were:

a project-level README that states a research purpose or links to a paper;
R scripts grouped in a recognizable location;
rendered Quarto or HTML documentation for project outputs;
links to OSF or another external archive for large or supporting material;
explicit configuration files, manifests, workflow orchestration, or batch scripts in the more computationally intensive projects;
project-specific assets such as spatial layers, sequences, model objects, or rendered figures kept near the workflow that uses them;
demonstrable links between public code, supporting information, and publications in several projects.

These are observed practices, not proof that the underlying analyses are correct. The proposed standard treats purpose, provenance, traceability, and handoff as the common layer and leaves tools proportional to project needs.
